\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\textwidth]{figures/factorial_2x2.pdf}
\caption{$2{\times}2$ factorial comparison: normalization type (BN/LN) $\times$ gradient flow (ON/OFF). BN row: gradient flow is associated with 4.2\,pp lower CAR ($g{=}0.78$); LN row: 2.9\,pp ($g{=}0.94$). All LN cells exceed Vanilla baseline. Error bars: cross-seed SD ($n{=}3$--$4$ per cell).}
\label{fig:factorial}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Factorial Interpretation}
\label{sec:mechanism}

\textbf{Gradient flow is associated with lower safety under BatchNorm but not under LayerNorm.} The BN row provides the clearest signal: Full OCC (BN) is the sole factorial cell falling below Vanilla ($\Delta{=}-2.0$\,pp, $g{=}-0.34$ vs.\ Vanilla), whereas Detach OCC recovers above it ($\Delta{=}+2.2$\,pp, $g{=}+0.74$, large). In the LN row, Full OCC+LN already exceeds Vanilla ($0.773$, $g{=}+0.90$, large), and Detach+LN yields the highest cell mean ($0.802$, $g{=}+1.89$, very large). This is a practical threshold crossing---only BN + gradient flow is associated with reduced safety---rather than a statistical interaction (the factorial interaction is negligible, $\eta^2{=}0.005$). The numerical advantage of Detach OCC ($+$2.2\,pp, with 3 of 4 seeds exceeding the Vanilla mean) raises the possibility that detached auxiliary heads provide a mild regularization benefit, though larger samples are needed to confirm.

\textbf{Temporal dynamics.} Cutoff conditions illuminate when gradient flow's association with lower safety emerges. Cutoff@15K ($\Delta{=}+0.2$\,pp) performs near Vanilla, while Cutoff@25K shows a large deficit ($\Delta{=}-5.6$\,pp) and Cutoff@35K returns to baseline ($\Delta{=}-0.1$\,pp). Cutoff@45K ($\Delta{=}-1.6$\,pp, single seed) falls between these extremes. This non-monotonic pattern suggests that the critical window for gradient flow damage is not simply cumulative exposure but depends on the training phase. Reverse Cutoff (gradient flow only after step 35K) produces the worst supplementary result ($\Delta{=}-6.2$\,pp), indicating that late-stage gradient flow---when the model is refining action predictions---is associated with substantially worse outcomes than early exposure during initial representation learning. However, temporal conditions use 1--3 seeds under v1, so these observations are preliminary; targeted replication under v2 with more seeds would be needed to confirm the temporal pattern.

\subsection{Gradient Dynamics}

Gradient analysis (Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_analysis}) examines the mechanism through which auxiliary heads affect safety. All gradient norms are computed with respect to the L1 action loss (action tokens only).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/gradient_analysis_v2_combined.pdf}
\caption{Gradient dynamics (v2, seed 42, 200 batches at 50K). (a)~Action gradient norms: comparable across conditions, ruling out amplification. (b)~Auxiliary gradient norms. (c)~Action--auxiliary cosine similarity: near zero (${\approx}$46\% conflict rate), confirming orthogonality. (d)~Norm ratio vs.\ Vanilla.}
\label{fig:gradient_analysis}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Gradient orthogonality rules out direct conflict.} Action--auxiliary gradient cosine similarity is near zero across all encoder layers for both Full OCC and Random OCC (Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_analysis}c), with conflict rates of ${\approx}$46\%. Action loss gradient norms are comparable across Vanilla and gradient-flow conditions (Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_analysis}a), ruling out gradient amplification. Both patterns are identical for structured and random labels, consistent with the behavioral finding that spatial content did not detectably affect outcomes. (Random OCC gradient data were obtained under the v1 codebase; the v2 gradient analysis covers all four factorial conditions and Vanilla, each using a v2-trained checkpoint.)

\textbf{Mechanism remains open.} The observed effect pattern cannot be explained by gradient conflict (orthogonality rules it out) or gradient amplification (norms are comparable). The observed effect likely operates through indirect modification of the LoRA optimization trajectory under a dual-objective loss landscape. Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) analysis~\cite{kornblith2019similarity} confirms that encoder representations remain intact under all conditions (CKA $\geq$ 0.95 for both DINOv2 and SigLIP across all encoder layers), localizing the safety deficit to optimization dynamics rather than representational corruption. This high representational similarity is notable: even the worst-performing condition (Full OCC, BN) produces encoder features nearly identical to Vanilla, yet its action policy exhibits substantially worse collision avoidance. The dissociation between representational similarity and behavioral safety suggests that subtle differences in the LoRA adapter weights---invisible to CKA---drive the safety deficit.

Additional observations (per-task breakdown, depth comparison, and temporal dynamics) are in Appendix~\ref{app:additional}.

\section{Related Work}

\subsection{VLA Safety and Spatial Representations}

Existing VLA safety approaches filter actions via control barrier functions or embed constraints during training~\cite{hu2025vlsa, zhai2026cofreevla, raja2024ogmcbf, zhang2025safevla}; none examines whether spatial representations contribute to safety. A parallel line enriches VLAs with 3D structure (depth fusion, representation alignment, geometric encoding, or occupancy prediction)~\cite{yuan2025depthvla, li2025qdepthvla, rao2026augvla3d, bhat20253dcavla, abouzeid2025geoaware, yang2024depthanything, li2026spatialforcing, wu2025spatialvla, li2025pointvla, sun2025geovla, wu2026stvla, chen2026gstvla, tu2026sgvla, xiao2026stemvla, li2026corridorvla, liu2025occvla, li2022bevformer, sima2023occnet, tian2023occ3d}, yet none evaluates collision avoidance. The occupancy branch originated in autonomous driving: BEVFormer~\cite{li2022bevformer} introduced transformer-based bird's-eye-view estimation, and subsequent work formalized dense 3D occupancy as a unified scene representation~\cite{sima2023occnet, tian2023occ3d}. OccVLA~\cite{liu2025occvla} adapted this to robotics; our BEV head follows the same principle but serves as a controlled factor to isolate the safety effect. SpatialVLA~\cite{wu2025spatialvla}, DepthVLA~\cite{yuan2025depthvla}, and OccVLA all report task success improvements but do not include safety metrics. The assumption that spatial awareness should improve safety remains untested; we address this gap.

\subsection{Auxiliary Tasks and Multi-Task Gradient Dynamics}

Multi-task learning exploits shared representations~\cite{caruana1997multitask, ruder2017overview}, but auxiliary tasks risk negative transfer through optimization conflict or generalization failure~\cite{chen2018gradnorm, yu2020pcgrad, liu2021cagrad, navon2022nashmtl, jiang2023forkmerge}. PCGrad~\cite{yu2020pcgrad} and CAGrad~\cite{liu2021cagrad} assume gradient conflict as the primary failure mode; our analysis shows near-zero action--auxiliary cosine similarity, suggesting a different mechanism.

BatchNorm~\cite{ioffe2015batchnorm} couples representations to batch statistics, while LayerNorm~\cite{ba2016layernorm} computes per-token statistics independently. BN running statistics degrade under covariate shift~\cite{schneider2020bn, li2017adabn}, which becomes consequential when auxiliary tasks alter training distributions. Our factorial design varies the normalization scheme between BatchNorm and LayerNorm, directly testing whether these distributional sensitivities propagate to the primary action objective.

For VLA fine-tuning with LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora}, the interaction between auxiliary losses and low-rank adapter updates has not been studied. LoRA constrains updates to a low-rank subspace, which may limit the model's capacity to accommodate competing objectives; our factorial design isolates gradient flow from spatial content and identifies two additive main effects that challenge the assumption that auxiliary spatial supervision should improve safety.

